    <link rel="icon" type="image/svg+xml" href="/favicon.svg" />
    <title>About — Field Notes</title>
  </head>
  <body>
    <main>
      <header class="site-header">
        <a class="publication-mark" href="/" aria-label="Field Notes, home">
          <span class="mark-icon" aria-hidden="true">f.</span>
          <span>FIELD NOTES</span>
        </a>
        <nav aria-label="Main navigation">
          <a href="/#observations">Insights</a>
          <a href="/writing">Writing</a>
          <a href="/experiments">Experiments</a>
          <a href="/notes">Notes</a>
          <a href="/about" aria-current="page">About</a>
          <label class="language-switcher">
            <span aria-hidden="true">LANG</span>
            <select aria-label="Filter content language">
              <option value="all">All</option>
              <option value="id">ID</option>
              <option value="en">EN</option>
            </select>
          </label>
        </nav>
      </header>

      <article class="about-page" aria-labelledby="about-title">
        <header class="about-intro">
          <p class="eyebrow"><span class="eyebrow-rule"></span> A NOTE FROM THE AUTHOR</p>
          <h1 id="about-title">A little about<br />the <em>curiosity</em><br />behind the notes.</h1>
          <p class="intro-copy">
            I’ve always been drawn to the parts of a system that aren’t obvious at first:
            what connects one thing to another, what happens at the edges, and why something
            that looks simple can behave in unexpectedly complicated ways.
          </p>
        </header>

        <div class="about-body">
          <section class="about-section" aria-labelledby="common-thread">
            <p class="section-label">THE COMMON THREAD</p>
            <div class="section-copy">
              <h2 id="common-thread">Different fields.<br /><em>The same questions.</em></h2>
              <p>
                My work has moved through software engineering and telecommunications, then
                into data integration, analytics, and loyalty systems. Along the way, the
                questions have led into infrastructure and, more recently, AI.
              </p>
              <p>
                The tools and vocabulary change. The underlying interest hasn’t: understanding
                how complex systems behave once real people, imperfect information, and
                everyday constraints get involved. I’m interested in the connections between
                components, but just as much in the assumptions and decisions that shape them.
              </p>
              <p>
                That perspective tends to follow me from technical work into organizational
                questions. A system is never only its design on paper; it’s also how people
                use it, adapt it, and make sense of it together.
              </p>
            </div>
          </section>

          <section class="about-section" aria-labelledby="why-field-notes">
            <p class="section-label">WHY FIELD NOTES EXISTS</p>
            <div class="section-copy">
              <h2 id="why-field-notes">Not every useful lesson<br />has an <em>official home.</em></h2>
              <p>
                Some of the most interesting things I learn never become a report, a conference
                talk, or a headline. They might be a small experiment that changed my mind, a
                pattern noticed during a project, or a question that’s still too early to answer.
              </p>
              <p>
                Field Notes is a place to collect those observations, experiments, lessons, and
                ideas before they disappear into the next thing. Not everything here is a
                conclusion. Some of it is simply worth keeping around long enough to think about.
              </p>
            </div>
          </section>

          <section class="about-section interests-section" aria-labelledby="current-interests">
            <p class="section-label">CURRENT INTERESTS</p>
            <div class="section-copy">
              <h2 id="current-interests">A few things I’m<br /><em>thinking with lately.</em></h2>
              <ul class="interest-list">
                <li>Local AI and agentic workflows</li>
                <li>Knowledge systems and how ideas stay findable</li>
                <li>Data, analytics, and the stories behind the numbers</li>
                <li>Software architecture and the behavior of complex systems</li>
                <li>Human behavior inside organizations</li>
              </ul>
              <p class="interest-note">
                These interests overlap, shift, and occasionally lead somewhere unexpected.
                That’s part of what makes following them worthwhile.
              </p>
            </div>
          </section>
        </div>

        <footer class="about-closing">
          <p class="eyebrow">KEEP WANDERING</p>
          <p>
            If you’ve made it this far, you’re already in the right place. Have a look around
            the <a href="/writing">writing</a>, <a href="/notes">notes</a>, and
            <a href="/experiments">experiments</a>. See what stays with you.
          </p>
        </footer>
      </article>

      <footer class="site-footer">
        <p>Curated by Firman Syamsudin. <span>Stay curious.</span></p>
        <a href="/">BACK TO THE BEGINNING ↑</a>
      </footer>
    </main>
    <script is:inline src="/language-filter.js"></script>
  </body>
</html>

